A||||16||ChadMan||Jumping the shark||||5||
Z||000000||ChadMan||05-25-2001||10:36 AM||||I rediscovered a great old Web page, <A HREF="http://www.jumptheshark.com." TARGET=_blank>www.jumptheshark.com.</A>   When "Happy Days" was losing ratings and going downhill fast, it got cheesier and cheesier, and at its cheesiest, Fonzie went waterskiing and jumped over a shark in a cliffhanger episode.  So the folks at this Web site defined "jumping the shark" as the moment at which you know a TV show has peaked and will never be that good again.  For example, the Dukes of Hazzard jumped the shark when they brought on those two dip$hits to replace Bo and Luke; Scooby Doo jumped the shark when they added Scrappy.<P>I got to thinking that the term could apply to anything - especially to cars.  For instance, the MGB jumped the shark in 1974 when they attached those ugly black bumpers and emissions crap.<P>Do you think the Fiero ever jumped the shark?  What other good examples of automotive shark-jumping can you think of?||||reg||5
Z||000001||ferg||05-25-2001||10:47 AM||||I don't think the fiero jumped. <P>The pinto mustangs of the early 80's, and when they put a 4 cyl. in them. But I think the shark ate the person responsible for making that executive decision, and now they are back on track.||||reg||1
